luisa noibawa is a climate activist and  leading figure in the fridays for future  movement in germany and globally she  joins us today on dw miss noibawa thank  you for being with us  you've said recently that europe's  dependence on fossil fuels is the root  cause of the war in ukraine and that  europe should respond by radically  building up renewable energy what good  do you think that could do now in the  short term when i when i say that i'm  just looking at the situation right now  together with economists with  diplomats with wolf experts everywhere  who see that the dependency on  russian fossil fuels  is one  of the of the of the many things that  make poutine so powerful right now  um this isn't anything or this this  understanding of of the roots of of  putin's power hasn't to do anything with  the concrete actions that need to be  taken now we ukraine needs a solidarity  right now they need support from all  over europe they need what is needed to  protect them and that is very clear  yet while looking at the bigger picture  um we see it's important to to be honest  about who is financing parts of these  tax and that is germany for instance uh  you know who is you know getting half of  its energy from putin  we've  heard germany's finance minister  christian lindner who's not necessarily  one of friday's for futures best pals  say that renewable energy is the energy  of freedom do you think germany's  government shares your assessment in  terms of the need to push for renewable  energy now  well if they don't you know  get it now  that renewable energies aren't just the  greenest and cheapest kinds of energies  but also the most peaceful energy that  we have i wouldn't know what to wait for  i mean open your eyes it's out there um  it's a it's those fossil fuels imports  that  yeah that empower putin that finance  those rules and it's uh you know not too  hard to understand  that  democracies can't be truly free they  can't be truly sovereign and safe as  long as their energy supply depend on an  autocrat next door that has you know  obvious tendencies to start wars  we have a stark energy crisis on the  horizon  you call for massively building  renewable energies but now in germany  even green members of the government are  thinking of pushing back the phase out  of nuclear and coal it doesn't seem like  renewable energies are a viable solution  at least in the short term  energy experts tell different stories  and i think you know at this point it's  important to differentiate between  responses to a war and transformative  action that needs to be taken um you  know to get independent from fossil  fuels from putin and others  and we're seeing very clearly the  studies um tell a  very strong story about how of course we  can get 100 renewable  um yeah in the medium term and how  everything that we're doing now in terms  of energy action needs to have this  transformative character and this is not  the time to play games and this is not  the time to do some kind of symbolic  gestures  that fire and fuel conflicts like those  and of course at the same time and i  think it's important  to to highlight as much as possible  ukraine needs our support and that is  not just a question of energy diplomacy  where that's a question of solidarity of  peace actions of support that is needed  germany and other countries in europe  are however going to have to likely  bridge  the  decreasing amount of gas and oil or the  more expensive gas and oil  coming into the countries and it takes  time to build a big amount of renewable  energy sources is it acceptable then to  accept a pushback of of coal or even  nuclear at least in germany  well no of course not  we have  brought ourselves a government of border  valve in a very very tricky situation  where we are highly dependent on fossil  fuel imports from autocrats like poutine  and that has a cost  and the answer to that of course can't  be an increasing dependency on fossil  fuels but needs to be you know energy  independence and that is what we get  with the most democratic the most  peaceful the greenest the cheapest the  most  uh protecting kind of energy so that we  have and that is renewables and there is  a cost attached to that but cost is  attached to everything and the question  is just who bears a cost and how do we  handle it in a just way and that's of  course where the government is asked to  to make sure that those who can't afford  it get access to to clean and cheap  energy  as quickly as possible but that can  never be an excuse to delay any kind of  action at the same time the ipcc has  spoken a very strong language yesterday  again the climate crisis is escalating  and this is another time to not play out  the crises against each other but to  connect the dots and see the bigger  picture here  what do you think of uh the german  government's commitment to massively  invest in the military with a bill of up  to 100 billion euros i'm a climate  activist i  am  having our intention of getting involved  in uh  military or geo um politics discourse  that we have here in germany what we're  seeing though is that you know many  experts  are wondering where exactly all this  money is supposed to go and  uh when we will be at the point where  the government makes this money or  freezes money for things that are  actually transformative that actually  contribute to a more safe and more just  and  yeah more sustainable  society  you and fridays for future want germany  to become climate neutral by 2035 do you  think that is an achievable goal  especially in light of the war in  ukraine and what that means for the  energy market  well when we're writing those demands  we're not just writing random wishlist  wish lists that we you know that we just  come up with on an afternoon we talk to  experts we see what is possible if you  really want if you provide the political  will of you if you organize people  around a transformation towards climate  justice and yes of course it's possible  of course we can be renewable by  2035 and just yesterday the government  kind of hinted that they're actually  planning to transform some part of the  energy sector to an extent that that  part will be 100 renewables by 2035 we  see again and again and again we don't  have a lack of  technical possibilities we don't have a  lack of knowledge we have a lack of  political will and an open sen of an  understanding what this crises are about  and so yes um we can get there and this  war um the situation right now it has  opened many doors we can't backlash um  you know 20 years back um you know  repeating all the same mistakes it was  that were made before or we can jump 10  years ahead and say we are stopping  these crisis we stop accelerating them  we we actually build those safe and  peaceful democracies that are  independent from fossil fuels that  provide real freedom for the people  everywhere talking about the ipcc its  latest report on how climate change  impacts the world was just released what  do you think of its findings  well it's shocking again and again and  again yet most of the things in this  ipcc report were clear a long time ago  these were the things that my grandma  went you know on the streets about and i  think when you stop you know treating  those ibc reports as a breakthrough  report that tell us about a new world  when we have been knowing and ignoring  all that for decades and so i think you  know if anything it's time now to  connect yeah to connect the dots and to  understand the ipcc tells us everything  we need to know to to radically act  um the war financed and funded by a  fossil fuel system is telling us about  the the conflict the war dimension  behind fossil fuels  and i wouldn't know what would stop us  now from from acting as needed  but uh climate change is a global  problem how can it be fought globally is  such a powerful energy player like  russia  is now basically considered a pariah  state how can one push climate change  forward as  a global problem when some countries  maybe have to be shut out of dialogue  just for now i'm i'm less worried about  you know the energies interesting in  russia which is a whole different story  i'm worried about those you know liberal  democracies around the world that claim  to have understood that we're in the  climate crisis and still invest in  fossil fuels still fund those autocrats  like putin still you know defend an  energy dependency on those fossil fuels  and those imports i'm worried about them  where are they right now and while you  know finger-pointing at anyone else  those countries forget that they're  being the excuse for everyone else right  now this accounts for germany that  accounts for many other big industrial  players and if they would have gets they  would get started if they would start  changing the paradigm that we are in if  they started being a leader a role model  in this it would be a completely  different story and it's it's us who  need to look at them right now  and of course and i think that's an  important dimension we're seeing right  now we need to discuss energy  partnerships we need to discuss how  richer industrialized countries can help  others to get away from fossil fuels as  soon as possible we need a  an energy diplomacy that's taking place  but that is focused on what is needed on  the timeline that we have on the few  years that we have left to get things  started do you have any concrete  proposals on that front what liberal  democracies and rich countries should do  uh to help the others go along  i think we need governments to become  clear and  you know honest  about not only the energy  infrastructure that they build  themselves but also the energy  infrastructure that they fund themselves  we see a huge tendency towards you know  big industrialized countries from the  global north to to be all cheery about  climate action to praise their own  targets as much as possible while  funding financing and ensuring fossil  fuel development and fossil fuel  infrastructure in the global south  that's not how it can work out so we  need honesty here we need transparency  here and we need governments to be  cooled out as necessary in their whole  attempts to greenwash themselves to some  climate change heroes while honestly the  the fact speak a different language  while emissions are rising  do you think we are now at a turning  point that this situation does present a  turning point for  a peoples and governments perceptions of  the climate crisis and how to tackle it  i don't know about that but i know that  people right now have the power  to  to fight for justice and peace and  climate justice and all of that has  never been as intertwined as  interdependent as just today we're  seeing that there is no climate justice  without peace and no peace without  climate justice and independence from  fossil fuels it's all out there and if  we want to we can make that the turning  point do you think though that uh people  especially in europe  will understand this and will care about  climate change in the face of the threat  of of war looming so close  yeah again i think it's in our hands um  this is not about you know  claiming one crisis prior to the other  but about connecting the dots and  understanding what are the root causes  of the fossil fuel conflicts and wars  we're seeing not just in the ukraine but  all over the world this is not a new  story it's been out there it's just been  widely ignored especially by western  media and society when we are you know  bold about this  um  and yeah again we are we are able to to  connect the dots and to  to look at  transformative solutions  while supporting in the immediate  situation  but again we're seeing that also  governments and societies have a  tendency to ignore what's right in front  of them that's the story about the  climate crisis for the past decades so  we need to understand that we are needed  here and that we and our understanding  of the crisis and our experience of  people power are needed to make those  changes and to fight for those changes  needed  louisa neubauer thank you very much for  your time  thank you  you
 

